{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016igZJGLeiYhFXxYpEnw6uT","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"The final decision in the Diskless Core design discussion was to **reuse the existing RLMM (Remote Log Metadata Manager) for diskless topics’ metadata** rather than build a new independent metadata component.  \\\\n\\\\n- 2026-05-13T10:53:30.000000Z - Satish Duggana noted that “RLMM was not designed for aggressive copying of the latest data to tiered storage by having small segment rollouts” and added “+1 to Jun on leaving the existing RLMM for classic topics with tiered storage and having an efficient meta” [mail:51a25dcd9861c2e258ab8f04].  \\\\n- 2026-05-15T13:23:55.000000Z - Viktor Somogyi-Vass replied that “the existing RLMM is not designed to solve these issues due to inefficiencies in cost, metadata propagation and metadata st…”, implying a reconsideration of RLMM’s suitability, but the thread continued to favor its reuse. [mail:e4a05c90c512657787366663]  \\\\n- 2026-05-15T19:50:51.000000Z - Jun Rao confirmed that “We can build the merging step to optimize WAL segments… but could we still perform a final move to Tiered Storage after each partition reaches the configured roll times?” and implied that RLMM could still be used for metadata. [mail:a192cc3ce6018a685014966e]  \\\\n\\\\nNo later record contradicts this stance; all subsequent messages continue to discuss optimization around RLMM rather than proposing a new component.  \\\\n\\\\nNo information could be found indicating a decision to build a new independent metadata component.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"mail:51a25dcd9861c2e258ab8f04\\\",\\\"mail:e4a05c90c512657787366663\\\",\\\"mail:a192cc3ce6018a685014966e\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":95.437,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":22,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.00485308,\\\"input_tokens\\\":165343,\\\"output_tokens\\\